1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:06,000
Hello, I'm Georgie, and this BBC Learning\h
English video is all about phrasal verbs\h\h

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:15,680
for 'technology', so 'switch on'\h
your brains and let's get into it.

3
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:24,240
I need to set up an online bank account. I turn\h
on my laptop and log in with my password. I go\h\h

4
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:32,240
to the bank website and click sign up. I scroll\h
through the terms and conditions to make sure I\h\h

5
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:40,000
understand what I'm signing up for. After I've\h
set up the account, I sign out, close my laptop\h\h

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,760
and go and sit on the sofa. I switch on the\h
TV, but it's not loud enough, so I turn it up.

7
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:55,720
Great, let's look at those verbs in a bit\h
more detail. 'log in' and 'sign in' are\h\h

8
00:00:55,720 --> 00:01:03,560
synonyms. They mean 'to enter your username\h
and password to access a computer system'.

9
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:07,360
'I need to log in but I\h
can't remember my password.'

10
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:13,880
The opposites are 'log out' and 'sign out'\h
- 'to exit or end your computer session'.

11
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:19,440
'After checking my bank balance, I signed\h
out, so no one could access my information.'

12
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:25,160
'sign up' means 'register or create\h
an account for a service or website'.

13
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:30,400
'I need to stop signing up to so many\h
marketing emails. My inbox is full of them.'

14
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:36,040
'scroll through something' is this action\h
- 'we move through information or images\h\h

15
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:41,920
on a screen with a dragging or a swiping motion'.

16
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:45,040
'I'm sleeping really badly at\h
the moment. I think I need to\h\h

17
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:48,200
stop scrolling through social media before bed.'

18
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:53,520
'turn something on' and 'switch something\h
on' are also synonyms. They mean 'to give\h\h

19
00:01:53,520 --> 00:02:00,280
power to or activate a device or system'.\h
They can be separated, so you can say:

20
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:04,800
'I turned the TV on, or I turned on the TV.'

21
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,680
The opposites are 'turn something off' and 'switch\h\h

22
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:11,720
something off' - 'to stop the\h
power to a device or system'.

23
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:17,160
'Remember to turn off the lights when\h
you leave a room to save energy.'

24
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:21,920
'turn something up' means 'to\h
increase the volume or intensity'.\h\h

25
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:27,280
'turn something down' is the opposite -\h
'to decrease the volume or intensity'.

26
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,960
'I usually turn down the\h
brightness of my screens a\h\h

27
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:36,960
few hours before bed. It's better for your eyes.'

28
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:43,160
Finally, 'set something up' means 'to start\h
or prepare a device so that you can use it'.

29
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:47,760
'I just got a new phone, but I need\h
to set it up before I can use it.'

30
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:52,920
Remember, don't 'freak out' and stop\h
'scrolling through' social media. Instead,\h\h

31
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:59,840
'work on' your phrasal verbs with the free\h
worksheet on the website. See you next time.

